# Untitled

## Ultraselective Gas Separation by Nanoporous Metal–Organic Frameworks Embedded in Gas-Barrier Nanocellulose Films
Makoto Matsumoto and Takuya Kitaoka*
Selective gas-separation techniques are the key to achieving green chemical processes and sustainable eco-industries.[1] Especially, molecular-sieving membranes[2] have attracted much interest in low-cost and energy saving systems; they enable the precise separation of various gases based on their molecular sizes under moderate differential pressures. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are nanoporous materials consisting of metal centers connected by various organic linkers,[3] which have uniform cavities and high surface areas effective for selective gas separation and have potential applications to catalysis, drug delivery, etc.[4] Recently, various polymer composite membranes containing MOFs, such as MOF-5 in Matrimid,[5] Cu-terephthalic acid in poly(vinyl alcohol),[6] and zeolitic imidazolate framework-90 (ZIF-90) in 2,2-bis(3,4-carboxyphenyl) hexafluoropropane diahydride–diaminomesitylene,[7] have occupied much attention in practical gas separation.[8] However, the simple dispersion of MOF powders into polymer matrix frequently induces undesirable gas leakage at the polymer matrix–MOF interface because of the low affinity between them. ZIF-90 MOFs have excellent thermal and chemical stabilities; they consist of 2-imidazolecarbaldehyde (ICA) linkers and tetrahedral \( \mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II}) \) centers. ZIF-90 has an aperture of diameter \( 0.35\ \mathrm{nm} \) between \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) \( \left(0.33\ \mathrm{nm}\right) \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) \( \left(0.38\ \mathrm{nm}\right) \) in kinetic diameter;[9] the 3D structure is shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information). ZIF-90 has a carbonyl group, which interacts noncovalently with \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \).[10] Thus, ZIF-90 composite films can be used to separate \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) from \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4} \) mixtures in methane gas purification,[7] but the problem for gas leakage still remains unsolved.Cellulose nanofibers are promising natural nanomaterials for several applications.[11–14] 