Cellulose nanofibers are promising natural nanomaterials for  several  applications.[11–14]  2,2,6,6-Tetramethylpiperidine 1-oxyl (TEMPO)-mediated oxidation of wood pulp provides individually dispersed cellulose nanofibers (TOCN).[15] TOCN has a high density of carboxy groups on the crystalline surface.[16] Carboxylates have strong affinities for metal cations such as transition-metal ions, and form metal–carboxylate complexes.[17] We previously reported ion-exchange loading of metal cations and in situ synthesis of metal nanocatalysts on a TOCN
matrix.[18–21] In terms of gas separation, the ability of the highly dense structure of TOCN films to completely prevent oxygen permeation[22,23] makes such films potential matrices for MOFs. Here, we report the first in situ synthesis of MOFs on TOCN with carboxylate groups; the aim was to prepare high-performance gas-separation materials, based on strong interactions between the nanoporous ZIF-90 crystals and gas-barrier TOCN matrix, as shown in Figure 1.
