TOCN nanofibers (Figure 2f). During the initial stage of MOF synthesis, ZIF-90 crystal nuclei were possibly synthesized at Zn-associated carboxylates of TOCNs (Figure 1), and gradual crystal growth occurred to form MOF microparticles through nonsolvent-induced crystallization. Then, imidazole released a proton to form Zn-imidazolate with a \( \mathrm{ZnN}_{4} \) structure, followed by crystallization, as literature reported.[24] The \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) ions introduced beforehand into carboxy groups of TOCNs presumably acted as a point of contact for MOF crystallization, and many Zn-carboxylates on the TOCN surfaces could strongly interact with as-synthesized ZIF-90 crystals. EDS showed that a Zn-free TOCN film had a peak from Na \( \mathrm{Na}^{+} \)and \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) could have occurred at the carboxy groups of TOCNs (Figure S4, Supporting Information). Scanning electron
microscopy (SEM) and EDS mapping images of the cross section of the MOF–TOCN films roughly visualized a large number of cubic MOF fractions buried and uniform characteristic X-ray of the Zn element originating from the MOFs throughout the most area of the cross section (Figure S6, Supporting Information), plausibly resulting in the formation of the continuous percolation pathways for \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) from one surface to another across the film. As-prepared MOF–TOCN film and a paper filter were \( \approx 50 \) and \( 200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}} \) in thickness, respectively, confirmed by SEM imaging of the cross section of MOF–TOCN composite film@paper (Figure S6c, Supporting Information). The MOF–TOCN film layer appeared to attach in tight to the paper filter. EDS mapping image displayed the homogeneous yellow dots indicating the Zn element of MOFs (Figure S6e, Supporting Information), and thus many MOF fractions were estimated to exist throughout the film layer. The Zn content of the Zn–TOCN composite was \( 0.58\,\mathrm{\mol} \) per mol carboxylate (Table S1, Supporting Information); this is half the carboxy content of TOCN \( (1.18\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1} \) TOCN). Stoichiometric conjugation was therefore presumed, as reported.[25] The Zn content after ZIF-90 synthesis was \( 3.53\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1} \) TOCN; the
ZIF-90 fraction in the TOCNs had a net Zn content of \( 2.85\,\mathrm{\mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1} \) TOCN (Table S2, Supporting Information). The ZIF-90 MOF content of the MOF–TOCN composites was \( 44.2\,\mathrm{\wt\%} \), calculated from the Zn content of the pure ZIF-90 crystal; this is three times larger than the previous value, \( 15\ \mathrm{wt\%} \), reported for one of a ZIF-90/polymer,[7] resulting in the close packing of MOFs inside the film layers.
