The gas-separation behavior of the MOF– TOCN film@paper was evaluated from the permeances of \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) and \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \), using a standard apparatus (Figure S7, Supporting Information). Gas-separation tests were conducted under a steady state, and each permeance was kept nearly constant during \( 6\,\mathrm{h} \) measurements (Figure S8a,b, Supporting Information). A differential pressure used in this study \( (0.2\;\mathrm{MPa} \) feed with a back pressure of \( 0.09\ \mathrm{MPa} \) ) was sufficiently small, and thus the tendency for ZIF-90 MOFs to swell and distort their crystallographic aperture sizes was considered to be negligible under such mild conditions. Following of gas-separation tests was carried out at \( 0.5\,\mathrm{h} \) after the start of steady-state gas flow. The MOF–TOCN film@paper was subjected to gas-separation tests under different \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) and \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \) partial pressures, ranging from \( 25\% \) to \( 100\% \). The \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) permeance showed very high and roughly constant values in each inlet \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) partial pressure (Figure S8c, Supporting Information). On the other hand, the \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \) permeance in each inlet \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \) partial pressure was negligible (Figure S8d, Supporting Information). In general, gas permeance decreases with increasing feed pressure at the membrane using the solution-diffusion transport.[26] These results show that the MOF–TOCN film@paper can completely separate \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) and \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \) based on their molecular sizes, and no gas diffusion into the TOCN matrix occurs. The ZIF-90 aperture \( (0.35\ \mathrm{nm}) \), which is expected to remain unchanged under the mild testing conditions, is larger than \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) \( (0.33\ \mathrm{nm}) \) and smaller than \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \) \( (0.38\ \mathrm{nm}) \); in addition, the carbonyl groups of 2-imidazolecarbaldehyde in ZIF-90 would have a preferable affinity
for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) molecules.[10] It is therefore suggested that three factors contribute strongly to the high gas selectivity: (1) the high gas-barrier properties of TOCN films themselves, (2) the high permeance and affinity of nanoporous MOFs for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) , and (3) the strong affinity between MOFs and polar TOCNs.
The composite film almost completely prevented \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) permeation only from the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) gas mixture \(\mathrm{CO}_{2};\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) molar ratio, 1:1), as shown in Figure 3a. The gas flux, expressed in gas permeance units (GPU), of the original paper (Supporting Information) without MOF–TOCN films was about \(5.99\times10^{3}\) GPU for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(6.09\times10^{3}\) GPU for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) . As a matter of course, both gases passed through the paper substrate with micrometer-sized pores, resulting in no selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) . The MOF-free TOCN film@paper had high gas-barrier properties for both gases \((\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) : \(3.68\,\times\,10\) GPU, \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) : \(1.81\,\times\,10\,\,\mathrm{GPU})\) , as reported for oxygen.[12,23] The numerous hydrogen bonds between crystalline TOCNs must contribute to forming highly dense structure, leading to extremely high gas barrier property. Neither dissolution nor diffusion of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) gases occurred at the TOCN film layers. The Zn-TOCN film without MOFs gave a poorer gas-barrier performance because of some disturbance of the dense TOCN structure. In contrast, the in situ synthesized MOF–TOCN composite film@paper significantly reduced the \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) flux of \(2.83\,\times\,10\) GPU by over \(99\%\) , while keeping the \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) flux at nearly half \((2.97\,\times\,10^{3}\) GPU) that of the original paper. The \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) -barrier property of TOCNs, which is clearly shown in Figure 3a, presumably results from the molecular size of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) \((0.38\,\mathrm{nm})\) , which is larger than that of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) \((0.34\,\mathrm{nm})\) ,[23] although a very slight amount of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) \((0.33\,\mathrm{nm})\) passed through. The in situ synthesis of ZIF-90 therefore successfully formed connected nanoholes in the dense TOCN matrix, as expected from a large number of MOFs found in the cross section of the MOF–TOCN film, which possibly formed the continuous percolation pathways for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Such connective pathways for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) passing through would get opened by loading large amounts \((44.2\;\mathrm{wt}\%)\) and occupied volumes \((12.7\;\mathrm{vol}\%)\) of ZIF-90 MOFs (Table S2, Supporting Information) without gas leaking due to high affinity for TOCNs. A simple mixing of MOFs and TOCNs, without any in situ synthesis procedure, showed nearly no penetration of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) gases through a composite film of thickness \(\approx40\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) . The MOF–TOCN film has extremely
high selectivity, namely 123, for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) permeation based on the molecular-sieving effect of MOFs embedded in an ultradense TOCN matrix with high gas-barrier properties (Figure 3b).
The selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) permeation was calculated from each flux, using the literature method.[2] The original paper had no gas selectivity. In contrast, ZIF-90/TOCN composite film@ papers showed extremely high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivities (Figure 3b). The selectivity depended on the film thickness: thicknesses of \(\approx20\) , 40, and \(50\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) gave selectivities of 31, 93, and 123, respectively. These values are much higher than those previously reported for ZIF-90/polymer composite films, namely 37,[7] and other MOF–composite \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) -separation films, namely 27.[27]
In previous studies, various types of matrix polymers for MOF composites were investigated; these had gas-separation properties based on gas diffusion into the polymer layers.[7] Undesirable gas diffusion occurred in the polymer matrix, therefore \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) permeation could not be completely avoided. In contrast, TOCN films have good gas-barrier properties for most gases.[22,23] In general, gas permeation is caused by gas diffusion into the amorphous layer of the polymer matrix,[28] and strongly depends on the polymer matrix crystallinity.[29] TOCN nanofibers have high crystallinities, \(60\%{-}70\%\) , and inter-fiber bonding via multiple hydrogen bonds provides ultradense structures \((\approx\!1.43\;\mathrm{~g~}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) ,[30] which prevent the diffusion of most of gas molecules.[23] These TOCN nanostructures presumably contribute greatly to the high gas-barrier properties, and, in this case, neither \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) nor \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) passed through the TOCN film layers. We first achieved the formation of large numbers of nanoholes penetrating the TOCN matrix, by tailoring the MOFs-connected pathways for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) , enabling high permeance of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) through MOF–TOCN film@papers while keeping high selectivity, as shown in Figure 3. The permeance of \(\approx9.94\,\times\,10^{5}\,\;\mathrm{mol}\,\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\,\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\,\;\mathrm{Pa}^{-1}\) \((2.97\,\times\,10^{3}\) GPU) and the selectivity of 123 for our MOF–TOCN film@papers are far superior to those reported in the literature. Trade-off relationship between gas flux and selectivity has been reported as each upper bound in 1991[31] and 2008.[32] Thick gas separation membranes have higher selectivity but very low flux; in contrast, thin membranes provide higher gas flux but poor selectivity. As-prepared MOF–TOCN film@paper can overcome such difficult problem, and achieve high gas flux and high selectivity due to high affinity for TOCNs and high content of ZIF-90 MOFs.
In summary, ZIF-90 MOFs were topologically synthesized at carboxy groups on crystalline TOCNs in tight contact to prevent gas leakage at the interface. The combination of nanoporous MOFs and gas-barrier TOCNs gave extremely high flux of \(\mathsf{CO}_{2}\) and high selectivity for separation of \(\mathsf{CO}_{2}\) from a \(\mathsf{CO}_{2}/\mathsf{CH}_{4}\) mixture, and is expected to be useful for the design of high-performance gas-separation materials.
## Experimental Section
Materials: \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) and N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) were obtained from the Wako Pure Chemical Industries Co., Ltd. (Osaka, Japan). 2-Imidazolecarbaldehyde was purchased from the Sigma-Aldrich Corp. (St. Louis, Missouri, USA). An aqueous dispersion of surface-carboxylated cellulose nanofibers \((\mathsf{C O O^{-}N a^{+}}\) content \(1.18\;\mathsf{m m o l}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) ) was kindly provided by the Nippon Paper Co., Ltd. (Tokyo, Japan); the cellulose nanofibers were produced from softwood Kraft pulp by TEMPO oxidation.[15]
Synthesis of ZIF-90: \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) \((0.743\;\;\mathfrak{g},\;2.5\;\;\mathsf{m m o l})\) and ICA \((0.961\;\;\mathfrak{g},\;10\;\;\mathsf{m m o l})\) were added to DMF \((25\,\textrm{m L})\) , and the mixture was stirred at \(80~^{\circ}\subset\) for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) in an oil bath. After the reaction, the mixture was continuously stirred for \({\approx}30\) min to cool it down to room temperature. Methanol \((25\mathrm{~mL})\) was quickly poured into the mixture as a poor solvent, while continuing to stir vigorously. The suspended mixture was subjected to centrifugation at room temperature \((9200\,g,\,25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\,60\,\mathrm{min})\) to separate the product, and the powdery fraction was thoroughly washed with methanol under sonication. This procedure was repeated several times to yield a ZIF-90 MOF, shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information). The MOFs were stirred again in methanol for more than \(24~\mathrm{h}\) to remove DMF and other impurity molecules captured inside the synthesized ZIF-90 completely. After air-drying at room temperature, the MOFs were evacuated at \(170~^{\circ}\subset\) under reduced pressure. All procedures were performed according to the reported protocol.[17]
Preparation of MOF (ZIF-90)–TOCN Film@paper: An aqueous TOCN suspension \((0.5\,\,\mathsf{w t}\%\) , \(50\ \mathrm{g})\) and \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) \((0.089\ \mathrm{g},\ 0.3\ \mathrm{mmol})\) solution were mixed; then ion exchange between Zn and Na ions at carboxylates on the crystalline TOCN surfaces occurred immediately. The mixtures were subjected to centrifugation at room temperature \((9200\ g,\ 25\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C},\ 60\ \mathsf{m i n})\) , followed by washing the Zn–TOCN gels with acetone and then with DMF several times to exchange the solvent. Finally, the Zn–TOCN gels were poured into DMF \((25\,\textrm{m L})\) . The \(Z\mathsf{n}-\) TOCN composites were suspended in DMF containing additional \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) \((0.744\;\;\mathfrak{g},\;2.5\;\;\mathsf{m m o l})\) and ICA \((0.961\;\;\mathfrak{g},\;10\;\;\mathsf{m m o l})\) , followed by stirring at \(80~^{\circ}\subset\) for \(4\;\mathrm{h}\) . After cooling to room temperature, methanol \((25\ \mathrm{~mL})\) was poured into the mixture to precipitate the product. The synthesized MOF–TOCN composites (21.0 and \(33.7\ \mathrm{mg})\) were suspended again in methanol \((3.6\,\textrm{m L})\) , diluted over tenfold with methanol under vigorous stirring to remove DMF and other impurity molecules, and then filtered on commercial filter paper (Grade No. 2, Advantec, Japan) of area \(23.7\ \mathsf{c m}^{2}\) under reduced pressure. The wet MOF–TOCN film \(@\) papers obtained were put between two metal plates, and dried at \(40~^{\circ}\subset\) for \(4\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . The dried MOF (ZIF-90)–TOCN films of thickness \({\approx}20{-}50~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) coated on the paper were subjected to analytical characterization and gas-separation tests.
Characterization: SEM images were recorded on a Zeiss ULTRA55 (Tokyo Japan). EDS analysis was conducted on an NSS300 (Thermo Electron Co., Ltd., Aichi, Japan). XRD patterns were recorded on an XD-D1 (Shimadzu Co., Ltd, Kyoto, Japan).
Calculation of Gas Permeance: The permeance \((J)\) was calculated from the following equation in an SI unit:
\[
J=\frac{F}{A\times\Delta p}
\]
where \(J\) is the permeance, \(F\) is the gas permeability flow rate \((\mathsf{m o l}\;\mathsf{s}^{-1})\) , \(A\) is the membrane area \((\mathsf{m}^{2})\) , and \(\Delta p\) is the pressure difference between the two sides of the membrane \((\mathsf{P a})\)
\[
J(\mathsf{GPU})=10^{-6}\frac{\mathsf{cm}^3(\mathsf{STP})}{\mathsf{cm}^2\mathsf{s}\mathsf{cm}\mathsf{Hg}}
\]
In this equation, \(J\) is the permeance in GPU. The \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}\) selectivity was calculated from:
\[
\alpha_{\mathsf{CO}_2/\mathsf{CH}_4}=\frac{Y_{\mathsf{CO}_2}/Y_{\mathsf{CH}_4}}{X_{\mathsf{CO}_2}/X_{\mathsf{CH}_4}}
\]
\(X\) is the molar fraction in the feed, and \(Y\) is the molar fraction in the permeation.
Molecular Modeling: The crystal structure of ZIF-90 was graphically constructed using a free copy of Avogadro Version 1.1.1.
